  "content": {
    "title": "What holes make up Amen Corner at the Masters? See photos, history",
    "text": "Updated April 10, 2026, 2:19 p.m. ETAmen Corner is stunning.It's often considered the most beautiful piece of property on any golf course on the planet, a pristine area of flowering azaleas, a beautiful creek curving through each hole and only birds chirping among the golfers.Patrons sit far away from the holes, leaving golfers in their own world as they play the holes, a far difference from other spots at Augusta National and other tournaments around the globe.Here are the holes that make up Augusta National's Amen Corner.What holes are Amen Corner at the Masters?Amen Corner consists of holes 11, 12 and 13 at Augusta National.The tee box for Hole No. 12, one of the most famous Par 3 holes in the world, sits just behind the green of the 11th hole. The tee box for the Par 5 13th is just behind the green of the 12th hole.It's on the farthest end of the club's property, where no patrons can stand.The three holes are each of different pars, with No. 11 being a Par 4, No. 12 a Par 3, and No. 13 a Par 5.What are the Amen Corner hole names?Hole No. 11, Par 4: White DogwoodAugusta National description: At this hole begins Amen Corner, and wind is often a factor. A driver to the right side of the fairway is rewarded with a more level lie. A pond guards the green to the left and a bunker is strategically placed right-center. This hole may best be remembered for Larry Mize’s incredible chip shot in the 1987 playoff.Hole No. 12, Par 3: Golden BellAugusta National description: One of the most famous holes, this is the shortest par 3 on the course. With swirling winds, club selection can range from 6- to 9-iron. Rae’s Creek in front, and three bunkers, one in front and two in the rear, make it a necessity to land on the putting surface. The Hogan Bridge allows golfers to cross the creek.Hole No. 13, Par 5: AzaleaAugusta National Description: An accurate tee shot to the center of the fairway may allow a player to go for the green in two if he desires. A tributary to Rae’s Creek winds in front of the green, and behind the putting surface are four bunkers. The Nelson Bridge is located at this tee. From tee to green there are approximately 1,600 azaleas.Photos of Amen Corner 2026Hole No. 11Hole No. 12Hole No. 13Where did Amen Corner get its name?Writing for Sports Illustrated in 1958, Herbert Warren Wind described the holes as 'Amen Corner' because, as the Masters website describes, he used the term from a 1930's jazz song titled 'Shoutin' in that Amen Corner.'According to Wind in the Masters article, \"There was nothing unusual about the song,” Wind explained in a 1984 Golf Digest story, “but apparently the title was catchy enough to stick in my mind. The more I thought about it, the more suitable I thought the Amen Corner was for that bend of the course where the decisive action had taken place …”Ryne Dennis is the Deep South Connect Team Editor for Gannett/USA Today and former sports editor in USA TODAY and the Athens Banner-Herald. Find him on X @RyneDennis and email at rdennis@onlineathens.com.",
